1
00:00:00,490 --> 00:00:02,570
When you learn a squat from a video,

2
00:00:02,590 --> 00:00:04,320
you can watch the movement.

3
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:05,760
What's harder is seeing what

4
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:07,600
changes when it's done differently,

5
00:00:07,620 --> 00:00:09,180
and knowing where to look.

6
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:12,230
This is Coach's Eye:

7
00:00:12,250 --> 00:00:14,740
a spatial squat comparison Lens for SPECS,

8
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:17,660
recorded here in the Lens Studio preview.

9
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:19,750
A reference mannequin stands on a

10
00:00:19,770 --> 00:00:21,460
small stage in front of you.

11
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:23,220
It's a demonstration.

12
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:24,690
It doesn't track your body,

13
00:00:24,710 --> 00:00:27,280
and it doesn't assess your form.

14
00:00:28,810 --> 00:00:32,870
Pinch Start, and the reference squat plays in five phases.

15
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:37,370
One short cue and one highlight at a time.

16
00:00:43,190 --> 00:00:45,370
You can step back through the phases,

17
00:00:45,390 --> 00:00:47,780
and stop on the moment you want to study.

18
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:54,080
Then comes the core idea.

19
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:56,120
Choose one common pattern,

20
00:00:56,140 --> 00:00:57,940
and an exaggerated red version

21
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:00,180
appears over the white reference,

22
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:02,870
seen from the angle where the difference shows.

23
00:01:02,890 --> 00:01:03,310
Knees.

24
00:01:03,330 --> 00:01:05,310
The stage turns to face you,

25
00:01:05,330 --> 00:01:07,790
because this difference is sideways.

26
00:01:07,810 --> 00:01:10,790
In red, the knees drift inside the toes.

27
00:01:10,810 --> 00:01:12,290
In white, the reference:

28
00:01:12,310 --> 00:01:14,140
knees track over the toes.

29
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:17,660
Pinch it again, and you're back in the demonstration.

30
00:01:18,660 --> 00:01:19,420
Heels.

31
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:22,260
This time, the stage turns to the side.

32
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:23,590
The red legs fade back,

33
00:01:23,610 --> 00:01:25,560
so the heel stands out.

34
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:27,920
In red, the heels lift off the floor,

35
00:01:27,940 --> 00:01:29,990
while the white foot stays planted.

36
00:01:33,290 --> 00:01:33,730
Chest.

37
00:01:33,750 --> 00:01:34,780
Also from the side,

38
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:37,690
where a forward lean is easiest to see.

39
00:01:37,710 --> 00:01:40,130
The red torso tips far forward.

40
00:01:40,150 --> 00:01:42,760
The white chest stays up.

41
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:45,800
Every difference is exaggerated for clarity.

42
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:48,520
These are illustrative patterns to compare,

43
00:01:48,540 --> 00:01:50,160
not a diagnosis.

44
00:01:52,390 --> 00:01:54,030
Outside a comparison,

45
00:01:54,050 --> 00:01:55,970
you choose the angle yourself,

46
00:01:55,990 --> 00:01:58,440
and you can slow the movement to half speed.

47
00:02:03,240 --> 00:02:06,770
Pinch Replay, and it starts again from the top.

48
00:02:06,790 --> 00:02:09,010
On SPECS, the idea is to keep your

49
00:02:09,030 --> 00:02:11,140
hands free and your eyes up,

50
00:02:11,160 --> 00:02:13,100
while you study the movement in your own space,

51
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:14,460
and walk around it.

52
00:02:16,260 --> 00:02:18,970
The finish points you back to the comparisons.

53
00:02:18,990 --> 00:02:20,910
Whether it helps people learn,

54
00:02:20,930 --> 00:02:22,090
and whether the approach could

55
00:02:22,110 --> 00:02:23,730
work for other movements,

56
00:02:23,750 --> 00:02:25,680
is what we want to explore next.

57
00:02:26,950 --> 00:02:30,900
Coach's Eye. See the difference. Understand what to observe.
